on each side. [Little used in this sense.] (''d'') In hydroid polyps, the cavity of a vesicular marginal body connected by a canal with the gastrovascular system. (''e'') In echinoderms, one of the diverticula of the branched ambulacral canals; a sort of Polian vesicle of the ambulacral suckers.—{{smaller|'''Ampulla of Vater''', in ''anat.'', the sac-like space in the wall of the duodenum, into which open the common bile-duct and the pancreatic duct.}}

'''ampullaceous''' (am-pu-lā′shius), ''a.'' [< L. ''ampullaceus'', < ''ampulla'': see ''ampulla''.] Of, pertaining to, or like an ampulla; bottle-shaped; inflated.—{{smaller|'''Ampullaceous sac''', one of the hollow ciliated or monad-lined chambers of many sponges. See cuts under ''ciliate'' and ''Porifera''.}}
{{smaller block|Thus is formed one of the characteristic ''ampullaceous sacs.''
{{right|''W. B. Carpenter'', Micros., § 509.}}}}

'''Ampullacera''' (am-pu-las′e̤-rä), ''n.'' [NL., < L. ''ampulla'' (see ''ampulla'') + Gr. κέρας, horn.] Same as ''Amphibola''. ''Quoy and Gaimard'', 1832.

'''Ampullaceridæ''' (am-pul-a̤-ser′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Ampullacera'' + ''-idæ''.] Same as ''Amphibolidæ''. ''Troschel'', 1845.
